00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.947
Cecilia Ann from the Discord:

00:01:45.947 --> 00:01:47.577
"What's the deal with a sugar rush?

00:01:47.577 --> 00:01:49.980
"Does it really affect kids
and adults differently?"

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.780
Sugar rush, made-up thing.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.060
Grandparents used to see
kids eat a lot of sugar

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.890
and then be hyper and then
blame it on the sugar.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.430
That is the prime example of
correlation, not causation.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.440
Kids are hyper.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.070
After they eat they stay hyper.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.210
So it's not the sugar making
them hyper, it's their age.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:05.830
It's the fact that they
have high energy levels,

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.010
nothing to do with the sugar.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.790
There is some truth to
sugar causing a crash

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.190
because after you eat a lot of sugar

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.230
your body has to take
that sugar from your blood

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.560
and move it into your cells.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.870
It does that through insulin.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:16.990
When you get an insulin spike,

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.930
you kind of feel a little
sleepy, a little bit drowsy,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:19.910
a little bit more tired.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.130
A sugar crash is real; a
sugar rush is not real at all.

